            "answer": {
              "short": "Upward parabola with vertex $(0,1)$.",
              "worked": "Translate $y=x^2$ up by $1$. The vertex is $(0,1)$ and the graph passes through $(\\pm1,2)$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 70
          },
          {
            "id": "p10-q6b-original-domain-range",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "State the domain and range.",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "Domain: $x\\in\\mathbb R$. Range: $y\\ge1$.",
              "worked": "Every real input is allowed. Since $x^2\\ge0$, the minimum value of $y=x^2+1$ is $1$.\nDomain: $\\mathbb R$. Range: $[1,\\infty)$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 16
          },
          {
            "id": "p10-q6b-inverse-sketch",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "iii",
            "prompt": "On the same axis, graph the inverse.",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "sharedSolutionDiagramId": "p10-q6b-graph",
            "answer": {
              "short": "$x=y^2+1$, or $y=\\pm\\sqrt{x-1}$, with vertex $(1,0)$.",
              "worked": "Swap $x$ and $y$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=y^2+1\\\\x-1&=y^2\\\\y&=\\pm\\sqrt{x-1}\\end{align*}$$\nReflect the original graph across $y=x$, retaining both branches. The inverse is a relation rather than a function.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 0
          },
          {
            "id": "p10-q6b-inverse-domain-range",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "iv",
            "prompt": "State the domain and range of the inverse.",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "Domain: $x\\ge1$. Range: $y\\in\\mathbb R$.",
              "worked": "Swap the original domain and range. The inverse has domain $[1,\\infty)$ and range $\\mathbb R$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 16
          }
        ],
